/**
@page memory_ownership_semantics Memory Ownership Semantics

librdesc copies token `seminfo` by value internally. The `seminfo` is any
user-defined object; it may contain heap-allocated fields. The `token_destroyer`
callback is responsible for performing any cleanup required for those objects.

The ownership of each `seminfo` copy depends on the result of `rdesc_pump()`:

| Result | Ownership |
|--|--|
| @ref RDESC_CONTINUE | Parser took a copy of `seminfo`. The caller's original is no longer referenced and may be modified or destroyed immediately. |
| @ref RDESC_READY | Parser releases ownership of `seminfo` copies in the matched CST. `token_destroyer` is **not** called on them. The caller takes ownership and is responsible for destroying them during traversal. |
| @ref RDESC_NOMATCH | Parser retains ownership of all held tokens. `token_destroyer` is invoked on them upon `rdesc_reset()` or `rdesc_destroy()`. Caller owes nothing. |
| @ref RDESC_ENOMEM | Allocation failed mid-parse. The unprocessed token's `seminfo` is saved internally -- the caller must not destroy it. |

In *dirty* @ref ready_state_sec state, the parser still holds tokens in its
backtrack stack. The caller can:
- Call `rdesc_start()` to begin a new parse. The parser will call
  `token_destroyer` on any remaining held tokens before starting.
- Call `rdesc_destroy()` to release all resources, including any remaining held
  tokens.

To find out when the parser enters the *dirty* @ref ready_state_sec state,
refer to its documentation.

@note `token_destroyer` is set at `rdesc_init()` time and may be `NULL` if
      tokens require no cleanup.

@see rdesc_pump(), rdesc_resume(), rdesc_start(), rdesc_destroy()
*/
